## Approach

Explain how you approached the problem.

You can include:

* How you understood the problem
* What user flow you designed
* What features you decided to build
* How AI is used in your solution
* What makes your approach useful or different

Write your answer here.

I identified that teams waste significant time searching for information buried in documents and Slack threads. The solution brings AI-powered search directly into Slack — the tool teams already use daily. The user flow is: upload documents → documents are automatically chunked, embedded, and auto-tagged → users ask questions via slash commands, @mentions, or DMs → the bot retrieves relevant chunks via vector similarity search → an LLM generates a grounded answer with source citations and confidence scores. AI is used at three levels: (1) HuggingFace local embeddings for semantic vector search, (2) Groq-hosted Llama 3.3 70B for answer generation strictly constrained to retrieved context, and (3) LLM-based auto-tagging to classify documents on upload. What makes this approach different is the two-level out-of-scope detection (retrieval distance + LLM check), scoped access control (personal/team/org), and multi-turn conversation memory — all within a native Slack experience with zero hallucination.
